WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.170 --> 00:00:01.329
Good morning John.

00:00:01.329 --> 00:00:02.730
This is a QR code.

00:00:02.730 --> 00:00:07.710
It's a way of visually representing data,
just like barcodes, or snapcodes, or facial

00:00:07.710 --> 00:00:10.290
expressions, or the written word.

00:00:10.290 --> 00:00:12.600
And I was looking at one of these things recently
and I thought to myself: how much data could

00:00:12.600 --> 00:00:14.730
you really fit on a QR code?

00:00:14.730 --> 00:00:17.960
Well, QR codes are complicated and I don't
have enough time to explain them in total.

00:00:17.960 --> 00:00:25.529
But, suffice it to say that each block of
2 by 4 squares codes for one byte of data.

00:00:25.529 --> 00:00:26.840
One character of text.

00:00:26.840 --> 00:00:31.190
The question that I had was could you print
out all of Wikipedia on one QR code and have

00:00:31.190 --> 00:00:35.499
a camera that could scan that code and you
could get all of Wikipedia by scanning one

00:00:35.499 --> 00:00:36.499
QR code?

00:00:36.499 --> 00:00:39.579
I'm aware that this is a dumb question, but
it very much is the type of dumb question

00:00:39.579 --> 00:00:42.410
that I enjoy figuring out the answer to.

00:00:42.410 --> 00:00:47.370
Just the text portion of just the English
portion of Wikipedia is about 24 terabytes.

00:00:47.370 --> 00:00:49.909
That's 24 trillion bytes.

00:00:49.909 --> 00:00:53.179
So in order to get the number of squares we
need, we just multiply that number by 8 because

00:00:53.179 --> 00:00:54.219
it's eight squares per byte.

00:00:54.219 --> 00:00:57.159
I don't want to know how many squares this
thing is gonna be, I wanna know how many squares

00:00:57.159 --> 00:00:58.549
it's gonna be on each side.

00:00:58.549 --> 00:01:00.089
So, we take the square root of that number.

00:01:00.089 --> 00:01:03.600
To figure out how much space that will actually
take up, we have to figure out how big each

00:01:03.600 --> 00:01:04.890
one of those tiny little blocks are.

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.890
I'm gonna say about 2 millimeters because
that's pretty easy to print and to read by

00:01:08.890 --> 00:01:09.890
a QR reader.

00:01:09.890 --> 00:01:12.509
So you multiply that number by two and then
convert from millimeters to kilometers and

00:01:12.509 --> 00:01:17.390
there you have it, you have a QR code that's
just 27 kilometers on a side that has ALL

00:01:17.390 --> 00:01:18.700
of Wikipedia on it.

00:01:18.700 --> 00:01:20.159
Now, of course, this is just half the battle.

00:01:20.159 --> 00:01:25.790
We've printed out Wikipedia on a 27 kilometer
by 27 kilometer sheet of paper, but we need

00:01:25.790 --> 00:01:28.740
a camera that can actually do that.

00:01:28.740 --> 00:01:31.890
Well, if you have a camera with a 90 degree
field of view, then you can take a picture

00:01:31.890 --> 00:01:35.329
of this whole thing, you just have to be 13.5
kilometers away from it.

00:01:35.329 --> 00:01:37.229
However, there's some bad news that comes
along with this.

00:01:37.229 --> 00:01:40.900
First, at that distance, the atmosphere is
going to distort this image way too much,

00:01:40.900 --> 00:01:44.659
so we've got to take it out of the atmosphere
and into space, put the QR code on the surface

00:01:44.659 --> 00:01:48.409
of the moon and put the camera in orbit around
the moon at around 13.5 km up.

00:01:48.409 --> 00:01:49.409
No big.

00:01:49.409 --> 00:01:52.719
The second problem is actually a bigger problem
cuz it deals with you know, just sort of the

00:01:52.719 --> 00:01:54.119
fundamental nature of the universe.

00:01:54.119 --> 00:01:58.590
From so far away, and with blocks so tiny
to try and resolve, you run into what's called

00:01:58.590 --> 00:01:59.819
the diffraction limit.

00:01:59.819 --> 00:02:02.399
Basically, when passing through the aperture
of our camera, the difference between the

00:02:02.399 --> 00:02:06.139
size of our aperture, that's the hole that
the light comes through, and the wavelength

00:02:06.139 --> 00:02:10.459
of light is significant enough that it blurs
anything below a certain size.

00:02:10.459 --> 00:02:15.030
So to take a picture of something so tiny
from so far away, you need to do one of two

00:02:15.030 --> 00:02:16.190
things, or both of them.

00:02:16.190 --> 00:02:21.230
One, make the aperture of the camera really
huge or two, make the wavelength of the light

00:02:21.230 --> 00:02:22.230
smaller.

00:02:22.230 --> 00:02:25.140
But if you want to take the picture of this
thing with a cell phone camera, you can as

00:02:25.140 --> 00:02:28.780
long as your cell phone camera just happens
to be able to detect gamma rays.

00:02:28.780 --> 00:02:31.790
Gamma rays are radiation with a very small
wavelength, also problematically with very

00:02:31.790 --> 00:02:36.490
high energy, so it's difficult to print with
ink that would absorb gamma rays because they

00:02:36.490 --> 00:02:39.689
would normally just fly right through it,
so you've gotta find some kind of really special

00:02:39.689 --> 00:02:40.910
gamma ray absorbing ink.

00:02:40.910 --> 00:02:44.610
The other, in my opinion better, solution
to this problem is to make your aperture just

00:02:44.610 --> 00:02:48.650
huge and one way to do that is to just use
the James Webb space telescope which we're

00:02:48.650 --> 00:02:51.260
already developing and has a pretty massive
aperture.

00:02:51.260 --> 00:02:54.920
Now, you'd have to modify it so it could be
a wider-angle camera cuz it's a telescope

00:02:54.920 --> 00:02:56.920
right, it's got telescopic, not a wide angle
lens.

00:02:56.920 --> 00:03:01.010
But the James Webb space telescope does have
an aperture big enough that it could conceivably

00:03:01.010 --> 00:03:06.200
resolve these two millimeter pixel things
from 13.5 kilometers away.

00:03:06.200 --> 00:03:10.400
However, it does not have a sensor sensitive
enough to take a picture of all of them at

00:03:10.400 --> 00:03:13.260
once, which I would like for it to do so that
we can just take a picture and real quick

00:03:13.260 --> 00:03:14.260
have all of Wikipedia.

00:03:14.260 --> 00:03:18.320
So we are going to have to develop a new camera
sensor that has like 10,000 times more pixel

00:03:18.320 --> 00:03:22.709
resolution than the current highest in military
spy cameras that we use, but I have confidence

00:03:22.709 --> 00:03:26.349
that we can do that in the intervening time
between when we are able to actually print

00:03:26.349 --> 00:03:29.260
out all these pieces of paper, which is gonna
take some time, and get them to the moon and

00:03:29.260 --> 00:03:30.500
lay them out in the proper order.

00:03:30.500 --> 00:03:33.879
So the answer to the question "can you print
out Wikipedia and have it be a scannable QR

00:03:33.879 --> 00:03:34.879
code?"

00:03:34.879 --> 00:03:39.340
The answer is yes, not really with current
technology but it is possible.

00:03:39.340 --> 00:03:41.549
John, I'll see you on Tuesday.

00:03:41.549 --> 00:03:44.420
I want to end this video by saying you know,
I'm not entirely sure.

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:47.170
I could have got something wrong, and I want
to say thanks to Henry from MinutePhysics

00:03:47.170 --> 00:03:49.219
for coming over and helping me with the math
of this.

00:03:49.219 --> 00:03:52.150
That was a really enjoyable project.

00:03:52.150 --> 00:03:52.800
That's my life.

